xtrm{oz}$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, $1\\,\\textrm{ton}$ is equivalent to $2,000\\,\\textrm{lb}$." 13194139534662 17592186045417 false] [17592186198422 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431660 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198422 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431665 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198422 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431670 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198422 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431672 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198423 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431658 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198423 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431664 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198423 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431668 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198423 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431673 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198426 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431662 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198426 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431663 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198426 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431666 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198426 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431671 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198427 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431659 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198427 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431661 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198427 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431667 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198427 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431669 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198501 1050 420.0 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198659 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198659 1071 "A weight in a school laboratory has a mass of $1\\,\\text{kg}.$ What is this mass in grams?" 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198659 1071 "A weight in a school laboratory has a mass of $1kg$. What is this mass in grams?" 13194139534662 17592186045417 false] [17592186198659 1074 "A table showing the metric units of mass is given below.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\text{kg}$ | $1\\,\\text{kg}=1\\,000\\,\\text{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\text{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\text{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\text{mg}=1\\,\\text{g}$ |\n\nSo, $1\\,\\text{kg}$ is equivalent to $1\\,000\\,\\text{g}.$" 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198661 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198661 1074 "A table showing the customary units of mass is given below.\n\n| Name | Symbol |\n| --- | --- |\n| Ton | $\\text{ton}$ |\n| Pound | $\\text{lb}$ |\n| Ounce | $\\text{oz}$ |\n\nTherefore, from the given options, the only unit of mass is $\\text{oz}.$" 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198662 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198662 1071 "A book has a mass of $1\\,\\text{lb}.$ What is this mass in ounces?" 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198662 1071 "A book has a mass of $1lb$. What is this mass in ounces?" 13194139534662 17592186045417 false] [17592186198662 1074 "A table showing the customary units of mass is given below.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ton | $\\text{ton}$ | $1\\,\\text{ton}=2,000\\,\\text{lb}$ |\n| Pound | $\\text{lb}$ | $1\\,\\text{lb}=16\\,\\text{oz}$ |\n| Ounce | $\\text{oz}$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, $1\\,\\text{lb}$ is equivalent to $16\\,\\text{oz}.$" 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198671 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186198671 1074 "A table showing the metric units of mass is given below.\n\n| Name | Symbol |\n| --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\text{kg}$ |\n| Gram | $\\text{g}$ |\n| Milligram | $\\text{mg}$ |\n\nTherefore, from the given options, the only unit of mass is $\\text{mg}.$" 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186199492 1064 "**Measuring** means finding a number that describes the size of something.\n\nThe **mass** of an object measures how much \"stuff\" (or **matter**) an object contains. The mass of an object is connected to its weight. We use **mass units** to specify an object's mass.\n\nThe most popular measurement system within the scientific community is the **metric system**. Within the metric system, the most common units of measurement are called **base units**.\n\nThe base unit of mass is the **gram,** which we denote using the symbol \"$\\textrm{g}$.\" The object below has a mass of exactly $1\\,\\textrm g$.\n\n![](images/dd/dd6ce9605969965944c1b9a7a87e4064bc1c64fb7195ee58b27e744a5e6dfd2b.png)\n\nWe can measure the mass of an object using **weighing scales.** To measure an object's mass, we first place it on one side of the weighing scales. Then, we put $1\\,\\textrm g$ masses on the other side until the scales balance perfectly.\n\n![](images/dd/dd4da91ec2ee27333843532cbf303da3dbe5eac388c8b14b538b8852159b0f76.png)\n\nIn this example, we have an object on the left scale and sixteen $1\\,\\textrm g$ masses on the right scale. Since the scales are perfectly balanced, the object's mass is $16\\,\\textrm g$.\n\nIt's convenient to define new units of measurement from the base unit. The most common variations for mass units are shown below:\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nLet's give some examples of each unit, going from the largest (or \"heaviest\") unit $(\\textrm{kg})$ to the smallest (or \"lightest\") unit $(\\textrm{mg})$:\n\n- A regular bag of sugar has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{kg}$.\n- A single postage stamp has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm g$.\n- A single grain of sand has a mass of around $50\\,\\textrm{mg}$.\n\n**Important**: Each metric symbol for mass ends in \"$\\textrm{g}$\", the base unit." 13194139534662 17592186045417 false] [17592186199492 1064 "**Measuring** means finding a number that describes the size of something. \n\nThe **mass** of an object measures how much \"stuff\" (or **matter**) an object contains. The mass of an object is connected to its weight. We use **mass units** to specify an object's mass. \n\nThe most popular measurement system within the scientific community is the **metric system**. Within the metric system, the most common units of measurement are called **base units**.\n\nThe base unit of mass is the **gram,** which we denote using the symbol \"$\\textrm{g}$.\" The object below has a mass of exactly $1\\,\\textrm g.$\n\n![](images/dd/dd6ce9605969965944c1b9a7a87e4064bc1c64fb7195ee58b27e744a5e6dfd2b.png)\n\nWe can measure the mass of an object using **weighing scales.** To measure an object's mass, we first place it on one side of the weighing scales. Then, we put $1\\,\\textrm g$ masses on the other side until the scales balance perfectly. \n\n![](images/dd/dd4da91ec2ee27333843532cbf303da3dbe5eac388c8b14b538b8852159b0f76.png)\n\nIn this example, we have an object on the left scale and sixteen $1\\,\\textrm g$ masses on the right scale. Since the scales are perfectly balanced, the object's mass is $16\\,\\textrm g.$\n\nIt's convenient to define new units of measurement from the base unit. The most common variations for mass units are shown below:\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nLet's give some examples of each unit, going from the largest (or \"heaviest\") unit $(\\textrm{kg})$ to the smallest (or \"lightest\") unit $(\\textrm{mg})$:\n\n- A regular bag of sugar has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{kg}.$\n\n- A single postage stamp has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm g.$\n\n- A single grain of sand has a mass of around $50\\,\\textrm{mg}.$\n\n**Important**: Each metric symbol for mass ends in \"$\\textrm{g}$\", the base unit." 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186199496 1064 "Another way to measure mass is to use **customary units**. The most common customary units of mass are **ounce**, **pound**, and **ton**.\n\nA table showing some of the most common customary mass units is shown below.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Ton | $\\textrm{ton}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{ton} = 2,000\\,\\textrm{lb}$ |\n| Pound | $\\textrm{lb}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{lb} = 16\\,\\textrm{oz}$ |\n| Ounce | $\\textrm{oz}$ | $-$ |\n\nLet's give some examples of each unit, going from the largest unit $(\\textrm{ton})$ to the smallest $(\\textrm{oz})$:\n\n- A small car could have a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{ton}$.\n- A small bag of rice has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{lb}$.\n- A single slice of bread could have a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{oz}$.\n\nCustomary units are not standard in the scientific community. However, they are commonly used in the United States and other countries." 13194139534662 17592186045417 false] [17592186199496 1064 "Another way to measure mass is to use **customary units**. The most common customary units of mass are **ounce**, **pound**, and **ton**. \n\nA table showing some of the most common customary mass units is shown below.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ton | $\\textrm{ton}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{ton} = 2,000\\,\\textrm{lb}$ |\n| Pound | $\\textrm{lb}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{lb} = 16\\,\\textrm{oz}$ |\n| Ounce | $\\textrm{oz}$ | $-$ |\n\nLet's give some examples of each unit, going from the largest unit $(\\textrm{ton})$ to the smallest $(\\textrm{oz})$:\n\n- A small car could have a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{ton}.$\n\n- A small bag of rice has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{lb}.$\n\n- A single slice of bread could have a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{oz}.$\n\nCustomary units are not standard in the scientific community. However, they are commonly used in the United States and other countries." 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186199514 1064 "Let's recall the table that relates metric units of mass.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\n**Important**: Symbols in the metric system follow a consistent set of **prefix rules**, which we explain below. If you find these difficult, *do not worry*! For now, you just need to remember the conversions shown in the table above. \n\nThe prefix rules are as follows:\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm k$\" stands for **kilo**, which means $1\\,000$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}.$\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm m$\" stands for **milli**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{1,000}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mg}=\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\textrm{g}.$ \n\nNotice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{mg}$ to itself $1\\,000$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{g}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{1\\,000\\text{ times}}{\\overset{⏞}{1\\,\\text{mg}+1\\,\\text{mg}+⋯+1\\,\\text{mg}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g}+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g}+⋯+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ 1\\,000\\times \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000\\times 1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} & = \\\\ 1\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{g},$ as stated in the table." 13194139534662 17592186045417 true] [17592186199514 1064 "Let's recall the table that relates metric units of mass.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\n**Important**: Symbols in the metric system follow a consistent set of **prefix rules**, which we explain below. If you find these difficult, *do not worry*! For now, you just need to remember 